A||||2||Rumor||Grease Fittings||||1||
Z||000000||Rumor||12-02-2001||12:28 AM||||Hey all,<P>While we had the GT up on the lift today, my buddy used a grease gun to pump some grease into the grease fittings. the grease oozed out the side from a few of the fittings - is that a bad thing? I suppose that means that there is a hole in the rubber boot. no big deal, right? Thanks!<P>-Rumor<BR>85 Silver GT<BR>with a OEM spoiler and rear sway bar installed today!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-02-2001||01:23 AM||||Not a big deal. They're supposed to do that. How else will moisture and old grease be evacuated? As long as the boot isn't cracked and dry rotted.<BR>Don't worry about it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-02-2001||10:50 AM||||The grease came out of where exactly?<P>As indy said, the grease often works out of the boots. It ususally comes out the "top" of the boots where the ball joint stud exits.<P>If it came out around the zerk fitting tip as you pumped it, then you either weren't on the zerk quite straight or the greas gun tip is getting worn. As long as some grease went in the zerk then no big deal.<P>One thing to avoid... you don't want the boots all puffed out tight. Running like that can make them tear.<P>If the old grease didn't exit by itself then I CAREFULLY slip a small DULL flat blade screw driver between the top of the boot and whever it's bolted into. That usually opens enough gap for the old greas to escape. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
